WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.705
Bounds errors happen when code reads from or writes to memory outside the intended object.

2
00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:14.833
The object may be an array, a dynamically allocated buffer, a structure field, a local workspace, or a region described by a pointer and length.

3
00:00:14.833 --> 00:00:21.425
The safety question is always the same: is this access inside the valid range for this object right now.

4
00:00:21.425 --> 00:00:25.356
Out-of-bounds reads access memory outside the intended object.

5
00:00:25.356 --> 00:00:32.265
They may return unrelated data, stale data, padding, or bytes that happen to be nearby in a particular build.

6
00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:41.330
Even when a read does not crash, it can corrupt program logic by feeding later code a value that was never part of the intended input or state.

7
00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:45.323
Out-of-bounds writes modify memory outside the intended object.

8
00:00:45.323 --> 00:00:52.993
That can corrupt adjacent state, break data structures, disturb length fields, invalidate pointers, or crash the program.

9
00:00:52.993 --> 00:01:01.677
A write beyond the intended region is especially dangerous because it changes the program's future behavior, not just the current result.

10
00:01:01.677 --> 00:01:08.270
Off-by-one errors often come from incorrect loop conditions, endpoint confusion, or terminator handling.

11
00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:13.214
For a zero-based array with count elements, valid indexes are less than count.

12
00:01:13.214 --> 00:01:18.349
A condition that permits index equal to count steps one element beyond the array.

13
00:01:18.349 --> 00:01:25.448
Strings add another common boundary: space for the content is not the same as space for content plus terminator.

14
00:01:25.448 --> 00:01:29.949
Lengths, indexes, offsets, and capacities must be validated before use.

15
00:01:29.949 --> 00:01:35.527
A start offset can be valid while the range from start through start plus length is not.

16
00:01:35.527 --> 00:01:40.535
A length can describe one buffer while code accidentally applies it to another.

17
00:01:40.535 --> 00:01:45.035
A capacity can describe total space while count describes current data.

18
00:01:45.035 --> 00:01:47.698
Defensive code keeps those facts separate.

19
00:01:47.698 --> 00:01:51.945
Check ranges before performing pointer arithmetic or memory access.

20
00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:59.805
It is easier to reason about offsets and lengths as values first, then derive a pointer after the operation is known to fit.

21
00:01:59.805 --> 00:02:05.954
A reviewed helper for repeated range checks can make this pattern consistent across the codebase.
